On Oct 27, 5:51 am, Patric Mueller <bh...@bigfoot.com> wrote:
> You surely can get by without doing it but the rewards
> from Sokoban are certainly not insignificant

That's all true.

Personally, there's only one level of Sokoban that
I really don't like, namely, the hard version of the
second Sokoban level.  My feeling about that
particular level is that there are two or three distinct
places where one typo can easily leave you in a
situation that is effectively unrecoverable without
breaking a boulder (something that I often don't yet
have the ability to do, so I'd have to leave and come
back after rooting around in the mines for a pick-axe,
which for some roles is still a very dangerous idea
at that point -- Tourists, for example, in general had
best stay out of the Mines until some time after
completing Sokoban).  If there were only one or
two *times* when I had to navigate past such
tricky spots, it wouldn't be such a big deal, but the
hard version of the second Sokoban level has you
pushing almost all of the boulders in turn through
a series of about three such points (four in some
cases).  Consequently, the whole level has to be
completed on pins and needles, making sure
never to inadvertently double-strike a key at
any time.

The hard version of the second level has a few
gotcha points as well, but not nearly as many.

> Just dropping Sokoban from the game is not a solution
> either as then you'd have a big hole in the early game.

Agreed.  Sokoban is an important option to have in
the early game.  Some players treat it as mandatory
to get there as quickly as possible and complete it
as immediately as possible (I tend to fall in this camp
personally).  Others like to put it off until they need
food, maybe do a level of it at a time.  Some players
skip it altogether, which is fine as a matter of personal
choice, but IMO doing Sokoban is an important option
that players have, which should not be taken away.

Actually, I'd kind of like to see a couple of additional
optional branches, but I haven't yet thought of any
specific ideas for ones that I'm confident would be
genuine improvements.  (I was toying with the notion
of a multi-level "military base" branch, but upon more
consideration I am not confident that it would really
add much to the game, other than a lot of C-rations
and a ton of junk armor, which are NOT two of the
major things NetHack really needs more of, IMO.
Besides, it's kind of redundant with Fort Ludios.
So I discarded that idea.)

> > I'm not even in favor of removing or outright
> > replacing Gehennom, and it makes Sokoban
> > look like a paragon of fantastic game design.
>
> > I prefer less radical forms of improvement --
> > incremental changes that leave NetHack still
> > recognizable as NetHack but make it better.
>
> The problem with that approach is that different people
> have different views about what is the essence of NetHack.
> If you want widespread agreement about a change
> beforehand, you mostly get UI improvements.

This is true, up to a point, but I think there is some
room for a bit of conservatism (or moderacy, at the
least) without complete stagnation.  You won't get
100% consensus on anything, of course, but it may
be that 50% agreement is better than 5%.

> > I think removing the luck penalties from Sokoban
> > is a more radical change.
>
> Why? It makes the game easier and not harder
> and it doesn't change gameplay.

By definition, if it makes part of the game either
harder *or* easier (in non-UI-related ways), it
changes gameplay.

I'm not completely against changing gameplay,
but I'm against changes that reduce the amount
of variety in the gameplay experience and make
different areas of the game more similar to one
another.  For example, I would be against a change
that makes the Gnomish Mines play out more like
the main dungeon.  Similarly, I would be against
a change that makes Sokoban play more like the
main dungeon.  Sokoban is supposed to be a
different experience from the main dungeon.
That's what makes it interesting, a change of
pace from the rest of the game.

> You can still brag about doing Sokoban properly

If I wanted to brag about stuff I'd be trying to do
a lot of conduct play.

But Sokoban is an interesting challenge in a
fundamentally different way from most of the
rest of the game, and I'd hate to see it gutted.

> > SporkHack is an interesting variant, but it is not a
> > particularly conservative one in terms of gameplay
> > changes.  It's fine that there are variants like that,
>
> If you read the design concept of SporkHack it's clear
> that conservatism towards gameplay is not intended.
> http://sporkhack.com/design_concept.html
>
> Indeed it's the opposite, trying to find new and interesting
> gameplay elements for a nethackish game.

Indeed.  I don't recall whether I'd read that document,
but it was clear to me that SporkHack was trying to
explore new and interesting possibilities.  Which isn't
a bad thing:  there are several things in SporkHack
that appeal to me, which I think might deserve some
consideration for more widespread adoption in other
variants.

For example, SporkHack has enhancements to
the level format that would allow for some additional
interesting variety from level to level and even from
game to game.  The level format enhancements
could be ported over to other variants with few
immediate repercussions (the existing levels are
all still possible) but would then open up a number
of options for future development.  There was,
for example, something that was discussed with
regard to the Convict role patch that would have
been easier to implement in SporkHack, due to
its better level format.

I think that's the way variants like SporkHack should
be viewed:  as a sort of testbed for ideas, some of
which may eventually make their back into vanilla,
and others of which won't.

On the won't side, SporkHack makes almost all
resistances a matter of degree, rather than things
you either have or don't have.  This is an idea that
appealed to me in principle, because it makes an
aspect of the game that most players deal with
almost entirely in the early game something that
lasts into the middle and maybe even the late
game.  However, upon further consideration, I
don't like the details of how this works in Spork.
Some things (e.g. reflection) *should* be binary,
either you've got it or you don't.  Others (e.g.,
poison resistance) perhaps should be as they
are in SporkHack.  I've concluded that others
(e.g., intrinsic telepathy, sleep resistance) would
be better if they eventually wear off unless you
keep renewing them.  I'd be interested in seeing
a variant that plays around with this, to see if
it works as well as I think it might.


